+## 8. Older Aaron — “childless and dead before 1820” is not established
+
+**STATUS:** partial/corrective  
+**REPO EVIDENCE:** C153, C155, C157.
+
+The project has evidence for an older Aaron Caton in Prince William in the 1790s and as a juror in 1813. His 1810 household contains only an adult male and adult female. C153 also cites a later Rappahannock deed calling an Aaron Caton deceased by 1833–34.
+
+**GRADE:** C/hypothesis for any relationship to target Aaron.
+
+**CORRECTION:** no children resident in 1810 does not prove the couple never had children later; absence as an 1820 household head does not prove death. The precise date **28 Feb 1825** appears in later handoffs but no supporting record citation was found in those passages. Treat it as unverified.
+
+**RESEARCH CONSEQUENCE:** keep older Aaron as a control candidate, not a preferred or excluded father. Open the 1833–34 Rappahannock deed and trace its recited trust/estate venue.
