+## 9. Older Boonville Thomas — useful lead, not an identity bridge
+
+**STATUS:** partial  
+**SOURCE:** Jim Thoma's 2013 transcription of the 1850 Cooper County census, as preserved in the 17 Sep project research note.  
+**ENTRY:** household 66, John P. Maddex household: Thomas Caton, 77, male, “Taylor,” born Virginia.
+
+A second Cooper entry in the same transcription is a different Thomas, about 40, farmer, born Kentucky, household 1038.
+
+**GRADE:** B/fact for the published census transcription; original manuscript still needed.
+
+**LIMIT:** age 77 implies b.c.1772–73, which does not neatly fit C159's current reconstruction of the 1810 Fauquier head as already 45+. Shared name, Virginia birthplace and tailoring warrant investigation, not merger.
+
+**RELATED DESCENDANT LEAD:** Jimmie Lois Caton Jones, “The Grandfather I Never Knew,” *Arkansas Family Historian* 37(3), Sep 1999, says Benjamin Franklin Caton (b.1842) was a son of Thomas Caton and Elizabeth Ann Lawrey, married in Fauquier 6 Dec 1828, with four children born in Virginia and later children in Missouri.  
+URL: https://argensoc.org/wp-content/uploads/afh/AFH373Sep1999.pdf
+
+Pleasant Hill Baptist Church Cemetery separately lists Elizabeth née Lowrey (1804–1883) as wife of Thomas William Caton.  
+URL: https://mogenweb.org/cooper/Cemeteries/PLEASANT%20HILL%20BAPTIST%20CHURCH%20CEMETERY.pdf
+
+These secondary sources provide a concrete Thomas–Elizabeth descendant tradition to test against primary records. They do **not** decide whether that Thomas is the 77-year-old tailor, the 1830 “Jun,” or another same-name man.
